# === UNDEPLOYED: still in home/, no longer deployed to ~/.claude/ ===
#
# WHY A SECOND SECTION: a path can need to stay in home/ for some reason other
# than being deployed, while still having to leave ~/.claude/ on machines that
# already have it — because chezmoi never deletes a target it has stopped
# managing. Such a path cannot be deleted from home/ the way a retired file is.
# Same pruner, opposite precondition.
#
# The case that created this section was home/ doubling as the plugin root
# (#48): a path moving to the plugin channel could not be deleted from home/
# without removing it from the plugin too. The plugin was withdrawn (#312), so
# that case is gone and no other has appeared. The section is kept because the
# distinction is real and the guard below is what makes it safe.
#
# CI RULE: an entry below this marker MUST still exist in home/. If it doesn't,
# it isn't undeployed — it's retired, and belongs above the marker. That
# inversion is the guard: neither section can silently absorb the other's
# mistakes, and moving the marker without moving the entries fails the build.
#
# ORDERING, AND WHY THIS SECTION IS EMPTY: an entry here is only correct once
# the chezmoi external has stopped deploying that path — which is a change to
# the `include` filter in pmgledhill102/dotfiles, not something this repo can
# make. Listing a path while chezmoi still deploys it makes every `chezmoi
# apply` write the file and the post-apply pruner immediately delete it again.
# That converges on the right end state, but it is churn masquerading as
# working. So: dotfiles narrows the include FIRST, then the paths land here.
#
# AND IT WILL NOT (#311). dotfiles declined the plugin cutover — it keeps both
# existing channels, the chezmoi archive external and cloud/bootstrap.sh, and
# will not narrow the include (pmgledhill102/dotfiles#392, closed as not
# planned). So commands/, skills/, hooks/ and bin/ are NOT candidates for this
# section: chezmoi is still their delivery route on personal machines, and an
# entry here would produce exactly the write-then-delete churn described above,
# on every apply. There is no longer a platform where that would sit harmless
# either — the pruner now has a PowerShell sibling for native Windows
# (pmgledhill102/dotfiles#407).
#
# The mechanism stays for a genuine undeployment: a path that must still exist
# in home/ for some other reason but must leave ~/.claude/. Nothing meets that
# description today, which is why the section is empty.
